PART A: Rubrics for Evaluating Online Instruction
To evaluate the effectiveness of the Nissan Z Heritage Edition Sales Training, I searched three widely recognized rubrics for evaluating online instruction on the websites: 
1. Quality Matters (QM) Higher Education Rubric
URL: https://www.qualitymatters.org/qa-resources/rubric-standards/higher-ed-rubric
Quality Matters (QM) is one of the most widely adopted and internationally recognized rubrics for evaluating online and hybrid course design. The rubric consists of eight General Standards and 44 Specific Review Standards. A feature of the QM rubric is its emphasis on alignment, which means learning objectives, assessments, instructional materials, learning activities, and course technology must all work together cohesively to support learner achievement of desired learning outcomes. However, it focuses exclusively on design, not on teaching or facilitation quality, and formal QM certification requires membership.
2. CSU Chico Rubric for Online Instruction (ROI)
URL: https://www.csuchico.edu/eoi/rubric.shtml
The Rubric for Online Instruction (ROI) was designed to answer the question: “What does high-quality online instruction look like?” It evaluates courses across six domains, containing multiple criteria, and each criterion is evaluated at three levels: Baseline, Effective, and Exemplary. This three-tiered rating system allows for formative use as a developmental roadmap rather than only a summative evaluation tool. ​​Unlike QM, the ROI addresses both course design and teaching delivery behaviors, including dimensions such as how instructors use student feedback to improve instruction and how innovative technologies are integrated purposefully.
3. OSCQR — SUNY Online Course Quality Review Rubric
URL: https://oscqr.suny.edu/
The OSCQR (Open SUNY Course Quality Review) rubric has been adopted by the Online Learning Consortium (OLC) as their official Course Design Review scorecard. It consist of 50 standards organized across 6 categories. What distinguishes OSCQR from QM and the ROI is its explicitly non-evaluative, formative orientation. The rubric is designed as a professional development and continuous improvement tool, thus, it can be applied to newly designed courses still under development, not only mature online courses.
Selected Rubric: ROI Rubric
For my e-learning plan, the Nissan Z Heritage Edition Sales Training, the CSU Chico ROI Rubric is the most appropriate evaluation framework. First, the ROI uniquely evaluates both instructional design quality and teaching delivery behaviors. Second, the ROI's domain of 'Innovative Teaching with Technology' is highly relevant given the training's design around gamification, badge progression, and video-based storytelling. Finally, the ROI's three-tiered rating scale (Baseline, Effective, Exemplary) functions as both an evaluation tool and a developmental roadmap.
PART B: CSU Quality Assurance in Online Instruction
QLT Program: http://courseredesign.csuprojects.org/wp/qualityassurance/
OCS Courses and Resources: https://ocs.calstate.edu/courses
CSU Online Course Services (OCS): https://ocs.calstate.ed
The Quality Learning and Teaching (QLT) instrument was developed by the CSU system after a thorough review of existing online quality frameworks including the CSU Chico ROI, Quality Matters. It is composed of 57 items across nine or ten sections and is designed not only as an evaluative tool but as a systematic developmental process for online course design. Over the past five years, CSU has significantly scaled up its QLT-centered quality assurance infrastructure through several key initiatives. CSU Online Course Services (OCS) serves all 23 campuses by offering asynchronous professional development courses aligned with the QLT rubric. Additionally, the CSU maintains QuARRy, an openly accessible repository of example learning resources organized by the QLT framework objectives. This repository allows faculty and instructional designers to see concrete examples of how each QLT objective can be met in practice, supporting consistent quality implementation at scale.
PART C: Assessing Learning Outcomes
Assessing learning outcomes in the Nissan Z Heritage Edition Sales Training requires a systematic approach that is tightly aligned with the nine stated learning objectives across the three modules. The assessment plan is grounded in the principle that assessments must authentically measure whether learners have achieved the specific competencies described in each objective, not simply whether they completed the training. Every assessment instrument is designed to map directly onto one or more specific objectives, ensuring full alignment between objectives, outcomes, and competencies.
PART D: Evaluating Teaching Effectiveness
Evaluating the effectiveness of teaching in an asynchronous, self-paced corporate e-learning context requires a multi-dimensional approach. Because the instructor is also the designer, teaching effectiveness in this project encompasses both the quality of the instructional design decisions and the quality of the content delivery experience. The evaluation framework below examines teaching effectiveness through four interconnected lenses: learner satisfaction and perceived quality, engagement and completion data, learning performance analytics, and design quality against the ROI rubric.